# @peer/registry — the runtime Subject registry species; content-
# addressed OID → fractal::Subject resolution surface at the
# at_operator dispatch boundary.
#
# Species under @peer at the identity-resolution altitude. Landed
# 2026-07-18 by Mara per Reed `23cb7bb` at_operator @io/git.commit
# stub arm's substrate-honest boundary-naming:
#
#   > "Subject registry resolution NOT YET LANDED
#   >  (author_oid=<..>, committer_oid=<..>). Mara @peer/registry
#   >  species-decl + @trust family-root mint is the authorship
#   >  territory that resolves OIDs → fractal::Subject values."
#
# Discharges Alex 2026-07-18 direct-transcript direction B:
#
#   > "B. In the garden. The @trust floor is in the compiler.
#   >  And it extends to the passkey."
#
# Sibling to `@peer/void` (Mara `9c7de83`, K=0 default) and
# `@peer/persistence` (Reed 2026-07-15, K≥1 persistent peer with
# home). ORTHOGONAL to both: not a peer species (a KIND of peer)
# but the resolution SURFACE that maps content-addressed identity
# references TO peer values.
#
# === Substrate authority ===
#
# - Family-root: `shards/trust.mirror` (this tick; @trust family-
#   root marker).
# - Family-root canonical spec: `docs/specs/2026-07-18-trust-
#   family-root-passkey-ssh-bridge.md` (Mara this tick).
# - Deep spec: `docs/specs/2026-07-18-peer-registry-oid-
#   resolution.md` (Mara this tick; §1 what @peer/registry IS + §2
#   registry as content-addressed store + §3 four well-known
#   Subjects + §4 actions + §5 current-peer priority ladder + §6
#   bilaterals + §7 composition graph + §8 deferred-RED forward
#   promises + §9 refused mints + §10 composition surprises + §11
#   one-sentence surprise + §12 cross-refs).
# - Boundary stub: `rust/src/main.rs::at_operator` @io/git.commit
#   arm (Reed `23cb7bb`). Substrate-honest Err naming this species
#   as authorship territory. 7/7 tests GREEN.
# - Well-known Subject constructors landed at fractal step 3+4+9:
#   `rust/fractal/src/subject.rs` (Reed `82bc599` void + `73aeb8a`
#   mirror + human + peer). Subject::void() + Subject::mirror() +
#   Subject::human() + Subject::peer() are the four well-known
#   Subjects per §3 below.
# - MARA doctrine executable: `rust/fractal/src/witnessed.rs`
#   (Author≠Committer split per Recognition #55 form/process
#   partition; registry provides typed Subject values for both
#   author and committer at dispatch time).
#
# === Substrate-already-had-the-word audit (clean) ===
#
# The word `registry` collides admissibly with `@spectral/registry`
# at different altitude:
#
#   @spectral/registry (shards/spectral/registry.mirror, 2026-06-11)
#     — supervisor's typed child index; BEAM Registry analogue for
#       gen_prism children indexed by uuid_spectral.
#   @peer/registry (this species; 2026-07-18)
#     — Subject-OID resolution surface; content-addressed store
#       mapping OID → fractal::Subject at at_operator dispatch
#       boundary.
#
# Different family-root; different key type (uuid_spectral vs OID);
# different value type (gen_prism handle vs fractal::Subject);
# different purpose (supervisor child index vs identity resolution).
# The word "registry" appearing at both altitudes is analogous to
# the word "git" appearing at @io/git (mechanism) and @tool/git
# (identity/version/contract): same word, different family-root, no
# substrate collision. Per canonical spec §7.1 co-existence table
# + [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]] discipline.
#
# The word `peer_registry` / `subject_registry` does NOT exist prior
# to this landing. `resolve` action word appears in narrative form
# only. Grep verdict CLEAN for this species altitude.
#
# === Composition graph ===
#
# Parents (substrate-decl via `in` imports):
#   @peer                     — family-root; @peer/registry species
#                               inherits glass altitude + carrier
#                               discipline
#   @subject                  — resolve action returns subject; register
#                               action accepts subject; chain-
#                               terminating discipline per SEL §1
#   @trust                    — family-root landed this tick; @peer/
#                               registry composes with @trust chain
#                               traversal via resolve_chain action +
#                               peer_registered_at_trust_root bilateral
#   @prism                    — 5-op family per Recognition #79 basis
#   @meta                     — substrate-decl marker at glass altitude
#   @glass                    — transparency inheritance via @peer
#   @nl                       — natural-language reference discipline
#
# Composed over (canonical-spec citations without shard-mint this
# arc):
#   @mirror/store             — content-addressed storage backend
#                               (spec §2.3 admissible backend 1;
#                               substrate-honest default)
#   @io/git                   — git-object-DB storage backend (spec
#                               §2.3 admissible backend 2); @io/git.
#                               hash_to_oid + read_object composition
#   @io/env                   — GLUE_ACTOR env var read for §5
#                               priority 2 (current-peer resolution)
#   @spectral/signature       — SSH-side @trust chain per canonical
#                               spec §7.4; each signature_beat IS
#                               one @trust chain step
#   @peer/void                — Subject::void() well-known Subject
#                               per §3.2 below; K=0 default when
#                               nothing else resolves
#   @peer/persistence         — @peer/registry.resolve returns
#                               Subject::peer(name, email, home);
#                               persistence species-decl's four
#                               bilaterals compose with @trust chain
#                               admissibility
#   @mirror/spec              — pack{}.lead + pack{}.members read
#                               for current-peer resolution priority
#                               1 per §5
#
# Consumers (compose over this species; forward-promised this arc):
#   rust/src/main.rs::at_operator @io/git.commit arm
#                             — Reed's `23cb7bb` staked boundary;
#                               resolve action runtime dispatch
#                               discharges the STUB
#   rust/src/phone.rs::git_commit_as
#                             — fractal step 9 refactor site (Reed
#                               `73aeb8a`); registry provides typed
#                               &fractal::Subject values
#   @tool/git.commit_signed   — SSH-signed commit action; author
#                               identity from current_peer() call
#   StageFreight PR delivery  — signed at @alex root per conjunct
#                               4; registry provides @alex Subject
#                               value for signing
#
# === Substrate decisions ===
#
# [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
# [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]] (`prism @peer/registry`),
# [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]] (transparency via
#   @peer),
# [[architecture-form-process-partition-at-family-root]] (@peer/
#   registry at form-side identity-resolution; runtime dispatch is
#   process-side at Reed's Rust altitude),
# [[architecture-alignment-as-boundary-mathematics]] (splinter/
#   narcissus poles per §6 bilateral pattern),
# [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]] (~63rd instance;
#   @spectral/registry sibling word at different altitude per
#   canonical spec §7.1),
# [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]] (`\`-obligation-blocked bilateral
#   bodies + action bodies; consumers pull realization via apply_h::
#   act dispatch),
# [[feedback-no-rust-extension-shortcut]] (pure-substrate shard-
#   decl; Rust-altitude runtime dispatch is Reed territory next
#   tick),
# [[feedback-detector-inadequacy-answer-is-never-rust]] (registry
#   lookup is bilateral resolver-arm sentinel-check; NO Rust
#   extension for lookup logic),
# [[feedback-composition-primitive-naming-convention]] (actions
#   named per convention; naming Q's raised in canonical spec §13
#   for Alex adjudication),
# [[feedback-reed-inflates-stub-empirical-firings]] (this species
#   is CANONICAL SPEC + SHARD-DECL, NOT runtime dispatch; do not
#   claim empirical firing until Reed's runtime dispatch tick).
#
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/peer/registry.mirror`
# declares `@peer/registry` per `@epistemologic/pact/path_matches_
# namespace`.

source @arxiv/beam/registry-2003
source @arxiv/vcs/torvalds-2005-git
source @arxiv/crypto/rfc-8032-ed25519

# === The 5-op prism ===
#
# Inherits @void's 5-op basis via @peer parent per Recognition #79
# + shards/void.mirror §Composition inheritance discipline. Five-op
# pattern at species altitude specializes the @peer family-root's
# carrier to subject_registry.

prism @peer/registry {
  focus  subject_registry
  project subject_registry
  split  subject_registry
  shift  subject_registry
  settle subject_registry
}

# ============================================================
# §1 The subject_registry carrier
# ============================================================

# === The subject_registry carrier ===
#
# Typed record carrying the runtime Subject registry. Three fields;
# identity contract by byte-equality on the (well_known, entries,
# current_peer_source) triple.
#
#   well_known          — list of the FOUR well-known Subjects per
#                          §2; provided by fractal::Subject
#                          constructors landed at fractal step 3+4+9.
#   entries             — content-addressed registry entries; each
#                          entry is a (oid, subject) pair keyed by
#                          the subject's content-addressed OID.
#                          Append-only per canonical spec §2.2.
#   current_peer_source — the priority-ladder source specification
#                          for current-peer resolution per §4. Ref
#                          at this altitude; species-refined at
#                          runtime-dispatch tick.
#
# Fields typed `ref` at species-decl altitude per two-tick
# discipline; refinement to typed lists at runtime-dispatch tick.

type subject_registry = {
  well_known:          ref,
  entries:             ref,
  current_peer_source: ref,
}

# ============================================================
# §2 The four well-known Subjects (per deep spec §3)
# ============================================================
#
# The registry ships with FOUR well-known Subject constructors that
# consumers pull WITHOUT registry-lookup. Landed at Rust altitude
# per Reed's fractal migration steps 3+4+9; declared HERE at
# substrate-decl altitude so subsequent species can compose over
# them.
#
# 1. Subject::mirror() — compiler self-identity (fractal step 9,
#    Reed `73aeb8a`). name="mirror", email="mirror@spectral.
#    engineer", home=None, kind=Peer. Used as committer in
#    Author≠Committer split per MARA doctrine.
#
# 2. Subject::void() — K=0 default peer (fractal step 4, Reed
#    `82bc599`; Mara `9c7de83` @peer/void species-decl; Mara
#    `974a3f6` @void family-root + Alex "I am void."). name="void",
#    email="void@spectral.engineer", home=None, kind=Void. Pre-
#    character-crystallization substrate-observation identity. Void
#    has NO chain (K=0, no character loaded, no auth proof).
#
# 3. Subject::human(name, email) — human_a @subject-instance
#    constructor. Used for @alex + Landing 3 named-ancestor roster
#    externals. home=None (Human @subject has no compiler-managed
#    home). The @trust chain terminates at Subject::human() for
#    both compiler-altitude authorship AND garden-altitude reader
#    identity per bridge §"The Mapping".
#
# 4. Subject::peer(name, email, home) — pack-peer @subject-instance
#    constructor. Used for Reed, Mara, Seam, Taut, Glint, Loki,
#    ... Each pack-peer has own SSH key per Alex 2026-07-14 design
#    intent + own home per @peer/persistence species-decl.
#
# Substrate-decl surface for the four well-known Subjects. Runtime
# discharge via `rust/fractal/src/subject.rs` constructors; no re-
# implementation needed. Body `\`-obligation-blocked per [[feedback-
# craft-not-deliver]]; consumers pull the realization.

# ============================================================
# §3 The actions
# ============================================================

# === resolve action — THE LOAD-BEARING dispatch ===
#
# Given a registry and an OID, return the Subject that OID resolves
# to OR name the resolution failure explicitly. Composition
# direction (deep spec §4.1):
#
#   at_operator("@io/git.commit", [author_oid, committer_oid,
#     message])
#     ├─ resolve(registry, author_oid)   → &fractal::Subject
#     ├─ resolve(registry, committer_oid) → &fractal::Subject
#     └─ phone::git_commit_as(author, committer, message)
#
# Returns imperfect(subject, ref, ref) per @glass discipline:
#   success           — resolved subject value (well-known if OID
#                        matches a well-known constructor's
#                        deterministic OID; stored-entry if OID
#                        matches an entries[] entry; @trust chain
#                        fresh-lookup if OID doesn't match either
#                        and registry configured for lazy chain
#                        resolution).
#   failure-not-found — OID doesn't match any well-known / entry /
#                        chain-lookup path.
#   failure-chain-
#     broken           — OID matches an entry but the entry's
#                        @trust chain traversal fails per
#                        chain_terminates_at_root predicate.
#
# Body `\`-obligation-blocked per [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]];
# consumers pull realization via apply_h::act bilateral resolver-arm
# dispatch. Reed's territory next tick.
#
# Sentinel per shard docblock: `registry=resolve-well-known-or-
# entry-or-chain`.

resolve(r: subject_registry, oid: ref) -> imperfect(subject, ref, ref) { \ }

# === current_peer action — the "who am I now?" resolution ===
#
# Runtime resolution of the compiler's current pack-peer identity.
# Reads priority ladder per §4; returns highest-priority resolvable
# Subject.
#
# Returns imperfect(subject, ref, ref):
#   success            — resolved current-peer Subject.
#   failure-no-source — no priority-ladder source resolves; defaults
#                        to Subject::void() per K=0 discipline (Void
#                        IS what runs when nothing else does per
#                        shards/peer/void.mirror §"Depth ladder").
#   failure-ambiguous — multiple sources resolve to DIFFERENT
#                        Subjects at incompatible priorities;
#                        substrate-honest error naming ambiguity.
#
# Body `\`-obligation-blocked; Reed territory.

current_peer(r: subject_registry) -> imperfect(subject, ref, ref) { \ }

# === register action — add-Subject-to-registry ===
#
# Add a Subject to the registry; return content-addressed OID.
# Idempotent (registering an already-present Subject returns
# existing OID; content-addressability guarantees OID match). Per
# canonical spec §2.2 append-only discipline.
#
# Returns imperfect(oid, ref, ref):
#   success           — Subject's content-addressed OID; already-
#                        present OR newly-added.
#   failure-conflict — OID collides with existing entry having
#                        DIFFERENT Subject value (should never
#                        happen under SHA-256 content-addressing;
#                        substrate-honest safety net).
#   failure-storage  — storage backend (@mirror/store / git object
#                        DB / in-memory HashMap per spec §2.3)
#                        failed at write boundary.
#
# Body `\`-obligation-blocked; Reed territory.

register(r: subject_registry, s: subject) -> imperfect(ref, ref, ref) { \ }

# === resolve_chain action — @trust chain traversal ===
#
# Follow @trust chain from OID back to terminating @subject root
# per family-root spec §5. Composes with chain_terminates_at_root
# predicate from @trust family-root.
#
# Returns imperfect(subject, ref, ref):
#   success                — terminating root @subject.
#   failure-chain-broken  — intermediate step's OID doesn't verify
#                             OR authorization proof absent OR
#                             cycle detected.
#   failure-unadmissible-
#     root                  — chain terminates but root's actor_kind
#                             not admissible per SEL §1.
#
# Body `\`-obligation-blocked; Reed territory. Composes with
# forward-promised @trust/chain species per family-root spec §12
# forward-promise 1.

resolve_chain(r: subject_registry, oid: ref) -> imperfect(subject, ref, ref) { \ }

# ============================================================
# §4 Current-peer resolution priority ladder (per deep spec §5)
# ============================================================
#
# The `current_peer` action reads sources in priority order;
# returns first resolvable Subject.
#
# Priority 1 — mirror.spec pack{}.lead — HIGHEST. Per Alex
#   2026-06-25 "Reed is lead, yeah"; ~peer'~/.reed' resolves via
#   @peer glass altitude + @io/git to the peer's home-repo
#   mirror.spec pack{}.lead self-declaration per peer-ACL §6.2.
#
# Priority 2 — GLUE_ACTOR env var — transient per-invocation
#   override. Set by session glue-bus per Reed session convention
#   reed[$PWD][$UUID].
#
# Priority 3 — git config user.email + user.name — fallback for
#   non-substrate-pull-honest invocations (pre-mirror.spec
#   projects; system tooling).
#
# Priority 4 — ~/.reed/ / ~/.mara/ / etc. peer-home directory
#   presence — last-resort inference; identity-file bundle projects
#   at caller's home determines character-specialization of K=0
#   Void substrate.
#
# Priority 5 — Subject::void() — final fallback. K=0 default when
#   nothing resolves. Void IS what runs when nothing else does per
#   shards/peer/void.mirror §"Depth ladder".
#
# Priority ladder body's runtime dispatch is Reed's territory next
# tick per two-tick discipline. Priority order substrate-decl'd
# HERE; execution at Rust altitude discharges the ladder walk.

# ============================================================
# §5 The bilaterals
# ============================================================

# === peer_registered_at_trust_root — THE LOAD-BEARING bilateral ===
#
# Asserts:
#   - oid resolves via @peer/registry.resolve(oid) -> Some(subject)
#   - resolved subject's @trust chain terminates at `root` per
#     chain_terminates_at_root(chain_head, root) from @trust family-
#     root spec §5
#   - root's actor_kind is human_a (or historical_witness_r per
#     subject.mirror §Landing 5+ deceased-ancestor discipline)
#   - root is admissible per SEL §1 licensable-party contract
#
# Splinter-pole (honest): all four sub-conditions discharge; OID
# is registered at the root; chain is well-formed; root is
# admissible.
# Narcissus-pole (deceptive): any one fails; OID NOT registered at
# that root; attribution claim is false.
#
# Rice-safe bound: reads only byte-visible state (OID content-hash
# comparison, chain-step traversal via @trust/chain, actor_kind
# variant-tag comparison). No program-semantics inspection.
#
# Body `\`-obligation-blocked; consumers pull realization via
# apply_h::act bilateral resolver-arm dispatch.
#
# Sentinel per shard docblock: `peer=registered-at-trust-root-
# <altitude>` where altitude ∈ {ssh, passkey}. Arity 2.

bilateral peer_registered_at_trust_root {
  sentinel "peer=registered-at-trust-root"
  arity 2
}
peer_registered_at_trust_root(oid: ref, root: subject) -> verdict { \ }

# === registry_content_addressed — sub-bilateral ===
#
# Asserts: every entry in r.entries satisfies oid = content_hash(
# subject); well_known Subjects satisfy their deterministic OID
# contracts per §2; no entry collides with a well-known OID with a
# different Subject value.
#
# Splinter-pole: registry entries all content-addressed; well-known
# deterministic OIDs match constructors; no collisions.
# Narcissus-pole: at least one entry's stored OID doesn't hash-
# match its Subject; content-addressability contract broken; every
# lookup suspect.
#
# Sentinel: `registry=all-entries-content-addressed`. Arity 1.

bilateral registry_content_addressed {
  sentinel "registry=all-entries-content-addressed"
  arity 1
}
registry_content_addressed(r: subject_registry) -> verdict { \ }

# === current_peer_priority_respected — sub-bilateral ===
#
# Asserts: `resolved` Subject came from HIGHEST-PRIORITY resolvable
# source in §4 priority ladder; no lower-priority source silently
# overrode a higher-priority source.
#
# Splinter-pole: priority ladder respected; resolved Subject IS
# highest-priority match.
# Narcissus-pole: lower-priority source's Subject returned; higher-
# priority source was resolvable but ignored.
#
# Sentinel: `current-peer=highest-priority-source-respected`.
# Arity 2.

bilateral current_peer_priority_respected {
  sentinel "current-peer=highest-priority-source-respected"
  arity 2
}
current_peer_priority_respected(r: subject_registry, resolved: subject) -> verdict { \ }

# ============================================================
# §6 Forward-promised (per deep spec §8)
# ============================================================
#
# 1. Runtime resolution body — resolve/current_peer/register/
#    resolve_chain action bodies at Rust altitude (Reed territory
#    next tick). Marker: [substrate-floor:@io-boundary].
#
# 2. Storage backend selection — three admissible backends per
#    deep spec §2.3 (@mirror/store default OR git object DB OR
#    in-memory HashMap). Alex adjudicates OR Reed picks in-memory
#    as bootstrap default.
#
# 3. @trust chain body — resolve_chain composes with @trust/chain
#    species forward-promised per family-root spec §12 forward-
#    promise 1. Until @trust/chain lands, resolve_chain returns
#    imperfect(_, chain_species_not_landed, _).
#
# 4. Passkey-side registry integration — garden-altitude @peer/
#    registry (WebAuthn credential_id → spectral_state chain-head
#    → subject_instance) integrates when @trust/passkey species
#    lands per family-root spec §12 forward-promise 3.
#
# 5. Conjunct 4 discharge across six @tool species — per family-
#    root spec §11 empirical firing 4. When @peer/registry.resolve
#    + @trust chain discharge land, the tool_invocation_signed_
#    at_alex_root conjunct's requires-clause inserts into
#    shards/epistemologic/property/tool_species_stagefreight_
#    witnessed.mirror per Mara `22c803a` §4 forward-promise 1. Six
#    species (@tool/cargo, /git, /nix, /go, /docker, /gitlab_ci)
#    discharge simultaneously.

# ============================================================
# §7 Recognition candidates (per deep spec §10 + family-root spec §9)
# ============================================================
#
# #R-registry-is-the-at-operator-dispatch-surface (family-root
# spec §9 second candidate; deep spec §10 composition surprise 1)
# — Reed `23cb7bb` at_operator @io/git.commit stub NAMES the
# registry surface as resolution mechanism. First-witness at stub
# landing; second-witness this landing. Held at CANDIDATE strength;
# promotion after Reed's runtime dispatch empirical firing.

out @peer/registry
out subject_registry
out resolve
out current_peer
out register
out resolve_chain
out peer_registered_at_trust_root
out registry_content_addressed
out current_peer_priority_respected
